1 Background
As the pivotal interface for contemporary in-vehicle interactions, the Heads-Up Display (HUD) revolutionizes the experience by replacing physical instrument clusters, fostering unparalleled convenience. The seamless integration of HUD enables drivers to seamlessly access vital vehicle and navigation data without the need to avert their gaze, thereby alleviating driver anxiety [1]. Furthermore, it enhances the overall driving experience through its immersive nature [2], offering a level of engagement that transcends traditional interfaces. The HUD’s capacity to present visual information tailored to human cognitive patterns and characteristics is instrumental in mitigating driving fatigue, a cornerstone of safe motoring. Consequently, delving into the human factors that arise from the interplay between the driver and the HUD interface holds immense potential for significantly enhancing driving safety. By conducting research in this realm, we can effectively optimize the HUD’s design to better serve the needs of drivers, ultimately promoting safer and more enjoyable journeys. Drawing upon Wang’s seminal research on cognitive load and HUD-related theory [3], this paper meticulously outlines an association model that intertwines cognitive load, drivers’ visual task activities, and key HUD design elements, as depicted in Figure 1. These design elements are comprehensively categorized into interface layout, information structure, interaction mode, and visual
