2 Icons In the Driving Environment
2.1 Effects of vibration on visual perception
Different from the fixed information display in the past, HUD is gradually applied to more dynamic scenes, such as moving vehicles, ships and airplanes, where drivers can obtain driving status information and environmental information from the HUD in front of them without looking down, which greatly improves driving safety. In the HUD display, information appears in the form of text (speed, RPM, mileage, fuel level) and images (alert signs, warning signs). Clear information display and clear information communication are very important for drivers, however, many existing design principles are based on design guidelines that are only applicable to stable and static environments, and the vibration of the HUD display in a moving vehicle can place an additional burden on the visual system, for example, the human eye feels motion blur when reading text in a dynamic environment. Lewis and Griffin [4] found that vibration had a significant effect on visual cognition regardless of condition by having subjects sit in a vibrating seat with and without pedals for a digital reading task. In 1986, Lewis and Griffin [5] built on
the original experiment by comparing different distances and vibration frequencies and found that when subjects were sitting at a distance less than 1.5 meters from the object being observed, the effect of vibration increased with decreasing observation distance, and the effect of vibration at 3.15 Hz became more influential with decreasing distance compared to 16 Hz. At observation distances greater than 1.5 meters, the effect of vibration on observation was independent of distance. The subject’s viewing angle vibrates while the monitor remains stationary, then reasonable visual acuity is maintained at about 8 Hz, and if only the monitor vibrates while the subject does not move, then visual discrimination begins to deteriorate at frequencies greater than 0.6 Hz. In terms of image information, Andersson [6] found in a study on a military HUD that the complexity of symbols had a significant effect on visual performance. The orientation of the symbols is also important for their readability. Symbols consisting of horizontal lines would be difficult to recognize during vertical vibration. Li [7] verified that the line width, font, and size of text in AR environments all affect human cognition, and that the color contrast and luminance contrast of the HUD information also affects human visual searcher task performance. According to the dual coding theory proposed by Paiviop [8], the human perceptual system consists of two coding modalities, Verbal Code and Nonverbal Code. Verbal Code responsible for processing logogen information, and Nonverbal Code responsible for processing imagen information. Logogen is the coded representation unit of information perceived in the form of language, organized according to associations and hierarchies; imagen is the coded representation unit of nonverbal things and events and situations, organized in the relationship between parts and the whole. Logogen elements are the coded representations of information perceived in the form of language, organized according to associations and hierarchies; imagen elements are the coded representations of non-verbal things, events, and situations, organized in the relationship between parts and the whole. For example, a person can visualize an elephant by using the word “elephant”, or he can mentally visualize an elephant by looking at a picture of an elephant and then describe it in words. For the human perception mode, no matter it is structural information or unstructured information, the
