In the experiment, subjects were required to perform a judgment task by combining observational information from pictures presented in the computer monitor and videos played in Hololens. After reading the introduction to the experiment and pressing space, a “+” blank screen focus would appear, and then a pseudo-randomized target stimulus video would start to play in Hololens, with one trial presented every 18 s. The first 3 s of the video for each trial prompted “find the target on monitor screen”, the subject needed to remember the target stimulus from the computer monitor and determine which design style icon it was. In the next 15 s, Hololens would present a picture of a randomized combination of icons, with each icon not repeating, and the subject needed to turn their attention away from the monitor to find the target stimulus in Hololens. Hololens to find the location of the target stimulus, and press the keyboard to record the location of the target stimulus. Eprime records the reaction time for each judgment, and subjects are required to press the corresponding keypad according to the position of the target stimulus in the chaotic combination and the design style, which corresponds to “F” for filled icons, “J” for linear, “L” for linear-and-filled, and “L” for linear-and-filled icons. “L”. For example, if the target stimulus was a solid icon, which appeared in the second row of the first line, subjects needed to press the numeric keys “F”, “1”, “2”, and the timing started from the time the target stimulus appeared by pressing the space to the time the third numeric key was pressed to stop the timing, and the timing stopped after the third numeric key was pressed. After pressing the third numeric key to stop the timing, subjects waited for the cue to appear after judging one trial and then continued to press the space for the next trial judgment, if the judgment was not completed in time, then press the space twice to skip and wait for the next trial judgment. Each experiment took about 35 minutes to conduct.
4 Experiment results
After organizing the experimental data, extreme values brought about by random variables and unstable disturbances in the subjects’ physiology and psychology were eliminated from the results, and after three-factor ANOVA, it could be seen that different design styles vibration frequency and vibration direction both had significant effects on reaction time (P<0.01), and there was a main effect. After Pearson correlation analysis, it can be seen that there
is a positive correlation between vibration direction and vibration frequency, between vibration frequency and reaction time, between vibration direction and reaction time, and between design style and reaction time. There is a positive correlation between vibration frequency and reaction time. From the graph, it can be seen that the lowest reaction time occurs at vibration frequency 5Hz, and the longest reaction time occurs at 15Hz, which indicating with the vibration frequency increasing, more subjects’ cognitive resources were occupied. In terms of vibration direction, the vibration recognition performance of horizontal icons is better than that of vertical vibrations, while the readability of random directional vibrations is the worst. Overall, in vibration environments, linear icons have the best judgment performance, followed by filled icons, and linear-and-filled combined icons have the longest reaction time. Combined with two factor analysis, linear icons have the best recognition ability in lateral vibration. Linear icons still maintain the best recognition ability in vibration frequencies of 5Hz and 10Hz, while linear and filled icons have strong recognition ability at 15Hz vibration frequencies.
5 Limitation and future work
The experiment in this paper is inspired by Gabbard ’s monocular AR visual search task [13], and the experimental design is improved in this study. Hololens used in this paper better restored the driver ’s observation effect in the binocular environment, provides the stereoscopic parallax cue during binocular observation, and restores the driver ’s real observation experience. The environment of Hololens is AR information, and the computer display is real environment information. Just as the driver needs to constantly switch attention between AR environment and real environment when driving a vehicle, using Hololens as an experimental tool is more realistic than developing a vibration simulation interface program on a computer display. However, the application of Hololens itself will bring several new problems. One is that the weight of AR glasses is an additional burden for most subjects. Some subjects reported that the wearing of Hololens caused additional fatigue after the experiment, which may interfere with visual judgment. Secondly, the difference between Holons ’ brightness and fineness and computer monitors may also lead to additional burdens, and the frequent transfer of participants ’
