6 Conclusion
This paper reports the driver ’s recognition efficiency whith icons in different design styles (filled shape, linear and linear-and-filled combined icons) in HUD under lateral vibration, longitudinal vibration and random vibration direction and three-level design vibration frequency (5Hz, 10Hz and 15Hz). The experiment restores the AR-HUD display effect in binocular vision environment through Hololens, which enabled a more realistic simulation of the binocular vision environment encountered by drivers using AR-HUD. The experimental conclusion is that, in general, based on the influence of vibration, linear icons are more suitable for the display environment of AR-HUD, while linear-and-filled icons will cause great difficulties to the driver ’s recognition, and compared with longitudinal vibration, lateral vibration has less influence on the driver ’s recognition. In terms of vibration frequency, the icon recognition of all design styles will decrease with the increase in vibration frequency. This study provides a reference basis for the selection of icon design styles in the HUD environment, and by selecting icons with better visual recognition effects in the vibration environment can effectively improve driver recognition efficiency and enhance driving safety.
